---
title: "Executable content"
description: "Executable cells against a warm kernel: what a language label decides, how to set up Python or R, and how an output becomes a block."
---

This is the heart of "code-block-enhanced HTML." A fenced cell tagged with a
language runs against a warm Jupyter kernel; its outputs become their own blocks
tied to the cell's id. Everything on this page is produced from its own `.tmd`
source.

## Code cells

```{python}
#| echo: true
import numpy as np
np.arange(5).sum()
```

On save, **only cells whose source changed (plus downstream cells) re-execute**,
and the kernel never cold-starts. `{python}` cells run against a warm kernel: point
the server at a Python with `ipykernel` via `TALIESIN_PYTHON`. A cell in any other
language, or with no kernel available, renders as highlighted source rather than
failing. The first cell of a session pays the interpreter's own start-up (booting
Python and importing what the cell reaches for); every edit after that reuses the
kernel that boot produced.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Setting up a kernel

Code execution needs a Jupyter kernel. A one-time Python setup:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install ipykernel numpy matplotlib   # + whatever your cells import
export TALIESIN_PYTHON="$(pwd)/.venv/bin/python"
```

Now `preview`/`build` run `{python}` cells against that interpreter. No kernel is
fine too: cells just render as highlighted source, and the preview shows a quiet
"kernel unavailable" note instead of failing.
:::

A cell that takes a while does not make you wait blind. **Output streams into the
preview as the cell produces it**, so a training loop printing an epoch line, or a
progress bar redrawing itself, shows up live beside a running-elapsed badge rather
than appearing all at once at the end. Nothing is required to opt in: print as you
go and you can watch it. Progress bars that redraw with a carriage return (tqdm and
friends) render as one updating line, not one line per frame.

The kernel is resilient: if it crashes mid-session the server detects the dead
process and respawns it on the next run, and a wedged cell is interrupted instead
of locking up the kernel. The cap is on **silence**, not on runtime:
`TALIESIN_CELL_SILENCE` (default 600 seconds) is how long a cell may produce no
output at all before it is sent SIGINT. The budget resets on every line a cell
prints, so a long-running job that reports progress runs to completion however
long it takes, with no configuration. (To bound total runtime regardless of
output, set the optional `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT`.) The dev menu's **Restart
kernel** action drops the kernel,
re-runs every cell against a fresh one, and reloads so `define` values
re-bind.

### `echo`, `include`, and `cache`: three different switches

These three boolean options look similar but control different stages, and it
helps to keep them straight:

| Option | Does the cell run? | Source shown? | Output shown? | Cached in `_freeze/`? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| (defaults) | yes | yes | yes | yes |
| `#\| echo: false` | yes | no | yes | yes |
| `#\| include: false` | yes | no | no | yes |
| `#\| cache: false` | yes | yes | yes | **never** |

`echo: false` hides the source listing only; the cell still runs and its output
still appears. Use it when the reader should see a plot or a table but not the
code that made it.

`include: false` goes further and hides *both* source and output, but the cell
**still executes** (kernel state is kept), so a later cell still sees its
variables. This is the option for a setup cell whose result you never want to
see but whose state you need downstream, not a way to skip a cell: there is no
per-cell "don't run" option, because the cells form one sequential program
against a single kernel, and skipping one mid-stream would leave the ones after
it referring to undefined state. To skip execution entirely, run the whole
document with `--no-exec` / `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC`.

`cache: false` is orthogonal to both: the cell runs and shows normally, but its
output is never frozen, and everything downstream of it re-runs and is likewise
never cached (a downstream output follows from this cell's *value*, which the
cache key cannot see: only its code). Reach for it whenever a cell's result
depends on something that is not the cell's own code: a data file it reads, an
environment variable, a network resource, the clock, or a sample without a fixed
seed.

```python
#| include: false
#| cache: false
# Runs for its side effects; nothing appears on the page. cache: false because
# the result depends on data.csv, which the cache key cannot see (below).
import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_csv("data.csv")
```

::: {.callout-warning}
## The cache key sees your code, not your data

The `_freeze/` key is built from cell code, every upstream cell's code, and the
interpreter's identity. That makes a stale hit impossible when you edit a cell or
anything above it. It also means the key is blind to everything a cell reads that
*is not* code: a CSV or Parquet file, an environment variable, a config file, a
URL it fetches, the clock, an installed library you upgraded in place.

Edit `data.csv` without touching a line of Python and the numbers on the page will
not change, because as far as the cache is concerned nothing happened. Mark any
cell with an out-of-band input `#| cache: false`, as above.

To force one fresh run without editing anything, use the dev-menu **Restart
kernel** or run with `TALIESIN_NO_CACHE`.
:::

::: {.callout-note}
`cache` also exists as a document-wide default under the `execute:` front-matter
key (`echo` and `include` do not: every real document says those per cell). A
per-cell `#|` option always overrides the document default. See the
[cell-options reference](../reference/cell-options.tmd) for the full option table
and the [Configuration reference](../reference/frontmatter.tmd) for `execute:`.
:::

### Captions: figures, listings, and tables

`#| fig-cap:` adds a caption *and* makes the output a numbered figure, so a
`#| label: fig-x` cell can be cross-referenced as `@fig-x`. Two siblings caption
the other kinds of output:

- `#| lst-cap:` captions a code *listing* (pair it with a `#| label: lst-x`
  to reference it as `@lst-x`).
- `#| tbl-cap:` captions a *table* the cell prints (pair it with `#| label: tbl-x`
  for `@tbl-x`).

```python
#| label: tbl-summary
#| tbl-cap: "Summary statistics by group"
import pandas as pd
from IPython.display import HTML, display

summary = df.groupby("group").mean().reset_index()
display(HTML(summary.to_html(index=False, border=0)))
```

### Publishing a table, rather than printing one

`#| tbl-cap:` captions whatever the cell **publishes**, and the shortest way to
write a table in either language publishes something you did not mean. Both traps
are quiet: the page builds, the lint is clean, and the defect is visible
only in the rendered output.

In Python, a bare `df` on its own line emits pandas' own repr: a table tagged
`border="1"` and `class="dataframe"`, a row-index column, and a `<style scoped>`
block. `scoped` was removed from the HTML standard and no current browser
implements it, so that style element applies to the whole page instead of to its
own table. `to_html(index=False, border=0)`, as above, emits plain markup that the
page's own table styling reaches. Keep the index when its labels carry meaning (a
`describe()` table, where they name the statistic) and drop it when it is a bare
row number.

In R, `knitr::kable(format = "html")` returns HTML as a *string*. A Jupyter R
kernel prints that string, so the reader gets the markup as text (`<table>` and
all, escaped) in a code block. The same call works under knitr/rmarkdown, which
splices the string in itself, and that is what makes this a trap rather than an
obvious mistake. Publish it as HTML instead:

```r
#| label: tbl-coefs
#| tbl-cap: "Fitted coefficients"
IRdisplay::display_html(as.character(
  kable(tidy(fit), format = "html")
))
```

`corpus/analyst/` uses both idioms, with a comment at each one saying why.

### Folded code

`#| code-fold: true` collapses a listing behind a summary (label it with
`code-summary`), so long setup code stays out of the way until clicked:

```{python}
#| code-fold: true
#| code-summary: "Show the setup"
import numpy as np
rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
draws = rng.normal(size=10_000)
round(float(draws.mean()), 3), round(float(draws.std()), 3)
```

### Writing a figure to disk

Inline `{python}` figures are themed for the web (transparent background, neutral
grey axes that read on light **and** dark), but that theming never touches global
`rcParams`, so a plain `fig.savefig("x.pdf")` in a cell already writes a clean,
print-ready figure (black on white):

```{python}
#| label: fig-loss
#| fig-cap: "Loss curve"
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt, numpy as np
fig, ax = plt.subplots()
ax.plot(np.arange(40), np.exp(-0.1 * np.arange(40)))
ax.set_xlabel("iteration"); ax.set_ylabel("loss")
fig.savefig("figures/loss.pdf")
plt.show()
```

Use a vector format (`.pdf`/`.svg`) for line art so it stays sharp at any size;
`.png` at a high `dpi=` for raster content (spectrograms, heatmaps). A cell runs with
its **document's directory** as the working directory, so a relative path lands beside
the source rather than wherever you launched Taliesin — create the folder yourself
(`os.makedirs("figures", exist_ok=True)`). Then in LaTeX:
`\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/loss.pdf}`.

An R cell writes its own file the same way (`ggsave()`).

## Live, browser-side content

`{js}` cells run in your browser rather than against the kernel: reactive inputs,
a Python-to-JS bridge for handing kernel-computed values to a `{js}` cell, and
full 3-D scenes (Three.js/WebGL) are all `{js}` cells with no kernel involved. See
[Interactive and explorable documents](interactive.tmd).
